<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Thinking traces vs. synthetic reasoning.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Synthetic reasoning also outperforms training Mistral on thinking traces from Qwen3 VL 235B A22B Thinking by +3.8 MMLBD-C (Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.1 Main results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Internalized Reasoning for Long-Context Visual Document Understanding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Interestingly, Table <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Thinking traces vs. synthetic reasoning. ‣ 5.2 Internalization and control token analysis ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Internalized Reasoning for Long-Context Visual Document Understanding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows that the thinking-trace model exhibits minimal difference between CoT-on and CoT-off evaluation; we hypothesize the thinking traces do not substantially affect performance. The model may have internalized the reasoning in the same fashion as our synthetic traces, but with little measurable impact. This is in line with our observation of the similar performance of Qwen3 VL 235B A22B Thinking and Instruct on MMLongBenchDoc.</p>
</div>
