<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 11: </span>A comparison of the text and visual branches of synthetic reasoning V1. We find that the text branch is slightly better than the visual branch (+1.1 VA) and significantly better on MMLongBenchDoc and MMLBD-C (+2.3 and +2.0 MMLBD-C respectively).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Effect of CPT</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One might question to what extent the CPT checkpoints which we merged into our synthetic reasoning models are responsible for our results. Thus, we provide an extended version of Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.1 Main results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Internalized Reasoning for Long-Context Visual Document Understanding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> in Table <a href="#A3.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ Appendix C Effect of CPT ‣ Internalized Reasoning for Long-Context Visual Document Understanding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>. We note that CPT checkpoints were also merged into the Plain Distillation models and as noted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, SFT and CPT did not compose well for significantly higher VA scores. Given this, we find that the gains of synthetic reasoning are distinct from the gains of CPT and from the SFT models from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
